          "Engage the 17% opposition directly on governance competence and fiscal management, not on ideology — college-educated affluent voters who have defected to Democratic alignment are unlikely to return on conservative social messaging, but they can be impressed by a legislator who demonstrates fiscal discipline, transparent governance, and results-oriented problem-solving on local issues.",
          "Monitor property tax burden and school funding mechanisms carefully; at $96,303 median income with 83% homeownership implied by 17.1% renters, property tax is the most direct impact on household finances, and any legislator perceived as allowing property taxes to rise faster than income growth will activate latent voter dissatisfaction among the homeowner majority.",
          "Avoid messaging that positions the 17% Democratic minority as outside the community's values or governance concerns — in an affluent, educated suburban district, framing that dismisses or alienates college-educated professionals will reinforce the defection dynamic and signal that the representative does not represent the whole constituency with equal seriousness."
