          "Lead with working-class economic concerns: at $56,473 median income with 32.3% renters and only 16.5% college education, healthcare costs, wage sustainability, property tax burden, and housing affordability are the material issues that drive voter behavior—not partisan rhetoric. The district will respond to candidates who demonstrate they understand and can address these specific pressures.",
          "Build visible constituent service infrastructure: Lumsden's 82% appears to rest on the absence of opposition, not on a deep constituent relationship infrastructure. A challenger or primary opponent who establishes town halls, constituent service visibility, and a record of delivering on household-level concerns can undermine this margin rapidly.",
          "Engage the Hispanic and Black communities (8.5% and 8.3% respectively) with specific economic and healthcare messaging: at $56,473 median income, both communities face the same working-class economic pressures as the white majority, but often receive less direct candidate attention. Specific engagement on jobs, wages, and healthcare access in these communities can expand the opposition coalition and shift the competitive landscape."
