          "The renter rate of 27.9% combined with a median age of 37.8 and moderate income of $69,218 indicates a constituency with meaningful housing cost pressure and limited wealth accumulation — people who are meeting current obligations but not building long-term security at the pace they would prefer. In this demographic context, property tax fairness, housing affordability, and wage growth relative to living costs are the material governance issues that determine whether constituents feel the district's representative is working for them or managing the status quo. The 11.66% Hispanic population is substantial enough that it cannot be treated as a secondary constituency; in a district where the incumbent ran unopposed, Hispanic voters' actual engagement with and perception of representation is unmeasured and potentially soft. An incoming challenger could credibly argue that an unopposed representative has no track record of competing for Hispanic community support, and the structural economic concerns of Hispanic households — both renter families and small business owners — are sufficiently distinct from the Anglo homeowner majority that they deserve specific legislative attention. The demographic combination of moderate income, renter presence, college-education below regional norms, and a growing Hispanic community creates a district that looks safe only because it had no challenger in 2024, not because it is immune to mobilization around economic and community-service issues."
