          { name: "Renter Constituency Engagement and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 24.7% renter rate—nearly one-quarter of the district—represents a constituency with fundamentally different economic stakes than the 75% homeowner majority: renters prioritize housing affordability, tenant protections, and rental-market regulation while homeowners prioritize property tax management and school funding. An unopposed incumbent risks neglecting renter concerns entirely, creating an opening for a challenger who addresses them directly. Specific legislative attention to housing affordability, rental-market transparency, or tenant protections demonstrates that the representative is governing for the full constituency, not just the homeowner majority—and such attention is particularly valuable now, before opposition emerges to highlight the gap." },
          { name: "Opposition Vulnerability and Challenger Risk in Next Cycle", tag: "careful", why: "While Smith won unopposed in 2024, the demographic profile—87% white, moderate income, 17.4% college attainment, 39.5 median age—is not so overwhelmingly Republican that a credible challenger with strong economic messaging cannot compete. Democratic candidates in comparable Georgia districts have run competitive races when offering specific healthcare, housing, or education platforms. The unopposed victory should not generate complacency; instead, it should generate a strategic assessment of which constituent concerns (healthcare access, housing costs, education quality) could activate persuadable voters or renters if a challenger addresses them. Early, specific legislative action on these fronts builds a record of delivery that is harder for opposition to overcome." },
          { name: "Cultural or Divisive Social Positioning Over Economic Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class district where median income is $71,633 and renters comprise 24.7% of households, constituents are more likely to evaluate on economic performance and healthcare access than on abstract cultural positions. An incumbent who defaults to culture-war framing or highly divisive social stances risks alienating the renter minority and economically stressed working-class homeowners who care more about cost-of-living relief than ideological purity. Opposition in future cycles will exploit this vulnerability by centering economic concerns; an incumbent who has built a record on cultural positioning rather than economic delivery is vulnerable to being reframed as out-of-touch." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Indicates Durable Support Rather Than Opposition Absence", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result because no opponent filed is not the same as a 100% result because 100% of voters chose the incumbent—and treating unopposed status as evidence of invulnerability is a strategic error that erodes constituent-listening capacity and legislative responsiveness. Districts with this demographic profile have swung in other states when opposition has articulated economic alternatives. An incumbent who operates as if the seat is safe, rather than treating the unopposed result as evidence that current opposition infrastructure is weak (not that constituent preferences are immobile), risks being caught unprepared when a credible challenger emerges and discovers unmet constituent concerns that Smith has not actively addressed." }
