          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Community Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 17.75% Black, 9.54% Hispanic, and 4.39% Asian residents, GA-HD-34 is a meaningfully diverse suburban district where the 42% Democratic baseline appears weak relative to demographic composition and college-attainment levels. Black and Hispanic professionals and families in this district have specific interests in education quality, economic opportunity, hiring equity, and institutional responsiveness that are not being fully represented by the current 42% opposition baseline. Active engagement with these communities around their material concerns — not as demographic outreach but as constituent service — could plausibly shift 3-5 percentage points of the district's composition from swing status to Democratic coalition." },
          { name: "College-Educated Constituency Skepticism Toward Partisan Extremism", tag: "careful", why: "A 57% college-educated constituency in a suburban professional district has higher sensitivity than the statewide average to messaging that emphasizes institutional breakdown, conspiratorial thinking, or rejection of professional expertise — whether from the left or right. The current 58-42 result may reflect some consolidation of this constituency around the Republican incumbent's personal brand rather than strong ideological alignment; if the Republican messaging environment or candidate profile shifts toward extremist positioning, this demographic is capable of swinging decisively toward a moderate challenger. A Democrat should monitor whether the Republican nominee's positioning creates an opening among college-educated professionals who value institutional stability." },
          { name: "Treating 58-42 as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 16-point margin in a district this wealthy, educated, and diverse is a working majority reflecting current incumbent performance and existing homeowner alignment — not a structural Republican advantage. In a district with 27.68% non-white population, 57% college-educated adults, and a professional-class income profile, partisan loyalty is weaker than in rural districts and persuasion more responsive to specific governance performance. Any candidate or strategist who treats this seat as safely held (if incumbent) or safely opposed (if challenger) is misreading the district's underlying competitive structure." },
          { name: "Relying Solely on Partisan Messaging in a Professional-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with $141,234 median income and 57% college-educated adults is inherently skeptical of purely partisan or ideological framing; these voters evaluate candidates on governance competence, institutional respect, and whether specific policy positions address their material interests. Any campaign strategy that leads with partisan identity or ideology rather than concrete answers to education quality, property tax sustainability, and healthcare cost management will underperform in this district relative to a candidate offering specific governance solutions grounded in the constituency's actual economic concerns." }
