          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.2% renters with $77,496 median household income in an urban district, housing cost represents the largest monthly expense for a substantial plurality of GA-HD-35's constituency. Younger renters, working families, and communities of color in the district face displacement pressure as urban land values appreciate; zoning reform, development incentives for below-market units, tenant protections, and property tax relief for long-term residents are the concrete policy levers that determine whether the incumbent is actually addressing the economic constraint that shapes daily life for voters in this district. A candidate who can demonstrate specific progress on housing affordability — not rhetoric, but actual units built, actual tenants protected — is defending the Democratic margin and extending toward 65%+." },
          { name: "Urban Service Delivery & Neighborhood Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a 32.5-year-old, educated, renter-heavy district, voters evaluate government primarily on whether basic services work: transit reliability, pothole repair, park maintenance, street safety, and permit processing efficiency. The Democratic 61% reflects competence on these fundamentals, but the Republican 39% includes quality-of-service skeptics who will migrate if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering results on practical governance. Attention to neighborhood-by-neighborhood service metrics, rapid response to constituent complaints, and visible progress on infrastructure quality are the operational foundation for holding and extending the Democratic margin in a district where ideology matters less than results." },
          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Community-Specific Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "GA-HD-35's diversity — 53% white, 23.51% Black, 13.72% Hispanic, 4.02% Asian — means that a generic Democratic message can obscure distinct community concerns: economic mobility and workplace access for communities of color, small business support and regulatory fairness for immigrant communities, educational opportunity for younger families, and community safety in neighborhoods with different demographic profiles and safety experiences. The 61% Democratic margin is built on multiple communities, and that coalition is durable only if each community experiences the incumbent as genuinely attentive to its specific concerns rather than absorbed into a broader urban progressive narrative. Community-specific constituent service and leadership visibility in multiple neighborhoods' civic spaces are essential to maintaining confidence across the full coalition." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Safe Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61% result in an urban district with 43.6% college education and high voter mobility is not a ceiling but a current position that requires active consolidation. Younger, educated, mobile voters are not inherited Democratic voters; they evaluate on results and move if they conclude the incumbent is underperforming relative to the district's needs. In neighborhoods with 46.2% renters, where people move frequently, constituent loyalty is rebuilt with each election cycle and depends on demonstrable progress on the issues that shaped the last result. Any incumbent who treats 61% as permission to reduce constituent engagement or postpone difficult decisions about housing, transit, or service delivery will find that result eroding in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Dismissing the Republican 39% as Ideologically Irredeemable", tag: "avoid", why: "In an educated urban district, the 39% Republican share likely reflects a mix of fiscal conservatives skeptical of government spending, quality-of-service evaluators frustrated with Democratic governance, and voters who prioritize different issues (public safety, development regulation) than the Democratic majority. A candidate who frames the opposition as simply 'conservative ideologues' or 'cultural reactionaries' is misreading the district's actual opposition and losing the opportunity to expand the coalition by addressing legitimate governance concerns. The margin between 61% and 70%+ is likely accessible to a Democratic incumbent who takes the opposition's service-delivery and fiscal-responsibility concerns seriously and demonstrates that Democratic governance can deliver on those dimensions, not through rhetoric but through visible results." }
