          { name: "College-Educated, High-Income Urban Constituency Economic Interests", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $112,278 median household income and 74% college-educated, GA-HD-52 is composed overwhelmingly of white-collar professionals and knowledge workers whose economic interests center on education quality, transit and infrastructure investment, environmental protection, and healthcare access. These are the material concerns that drove the 62% Democratic result, and sustaining that margin requires demonstrating effective delivery on these priorities through legislation, budget advocacy, and constituent service that shows the representative understands what this constituency values and is capable of achieving it." },
          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Community Growth and Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 17.23% Asian population and 6.96% Hispanic population together represent nearly a quarter of the district, and both groups are demographics that have experienced political realignment in other states when they perceived insufficient responsiveness on education, small business regulation, immigration, or public safety. At 62% Democratic in a district with these growth demographics, active engagement on issues specific to these communities — rather than absorption into a generic 'minority outreach' framework — is essential to preventing defection and maintaining coalition durability." },
          { name: "Renter and Young Adult Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "With 51.3% renters and 36.7-year median age, GA-HD-52 includes substantial numbers of younger professionals for whom housing affordability, student debt, and economic opportunity are direct material concerns. While the Democratic margin is currently secure, younger renters are a constituency capable of voting differently if they conclude that housing costs are rising faster than wages, that student debt relief is stalled, or that Republican alternatives offer better solutions to these specific pressures — particularly in a high-cost urban market where these concerns are acute." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe Majority Without Active Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62% result in a district composed of college-educated, high-income professionals is not a floor — it is a coalition that elected the incumbent to deliver specific governance outcomes around education, infrastructure, environment, and healthcare. Any representative who treats this margin as durable without demonstrating active delivery on these priorities is vulnerable to a challenger who can credibly argue that governance is stalled, that constituent concerns are unaddressed, or that an alternative approach is more effective." },
          { name: "Assuming the Coalition Is Monolithic or Self-Sustaining", tag: "avoid", why: "GA-HD-52's Democratic coalition is diverse by race, with no single group comprising a majority, and united primarily around shared economic interests and values-based governance rather than inherited partisan loyalty. This diversity is a source of strength, but only if the incumbent actively maintains the coalition through specific attention to the distinct priorities of different constituencies. Assuming the coalition will hold because it is currently Democratic, without actively serving its multiple component parts, is the primary vulnerability a representative in this district faces." }
