          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 42.4%, renters represent the largest unaddressed constituency in GA-HD-61, and younger median age (35) means this is not temporary — these are younger adults making long-term decisions about whether to stay in the district or move elsewhere. Housing affordability, tenant protections against displacement, zoning that permits mixed-income development, and pathways to homeownership through down-payment assistance and equitable lending are the state-level policies that affect whether renters can build wealth or are perpetually excluded from it. An incumbent who cannot speak with specificity about these issues is vulnerable to a primary challenger who can." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Integration & Economic Access", tag: "careful", why: "At 13.42%, the Hispanic population is substantial enough to be a decisive swing constituency in a primary contest, but small enough that it can be rendered invisible if the district's representative defaults to framing all minority community concerns as 'Black community issues.' Workforce development, small business access to credit, immigrant family services, and education equity are the specific policy areas where a representative must demonstrate sustained engagement rather than token acknowledgment. Neglecting this community or absorbing it into an undifferentiated 'communities of color' frame risks creating an opportunity for a challenger to mobilize Hispanic voters around responsiveness." },
          { name: "Dismissing Primary Vulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed general election result in a heavily Democratic district says nothing about primary vulnerability or long-term seat security. The 42.4% renter population, 35-year median age, and 13.42% Hispanic community are all constituencies that can organize around a primary challenger if they perceive the incumbent as unresponsive to their material concerns. Treating the 100% Democratic, 0% Republican result as a sign of electoral safety rather than as an opportunity to deepen constituent relationships is a strategic mistake that creates opening for primary opposition." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Identity Politics Without Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69.57% Black district may seem to guarantee Democratic victory, but Black voters in GA-HD-61 are economically diverse and evaluating on whether their representative is delivering concrete policy results on housing, small business opportunity, education, and professional advancement — not on racial identity alone. An incumbent whose messaging emphasizes symbolic representation without tangible policy achievements for renters, young professionals, small business owners, and immigrants is vulnerable to a primary challenger whose agenda is more specific to constituent economic interests. This constituency demands delivery, not just rhetoric." }
