        issues: [
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency Policy Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 28% college-educated adults and $92,338 median household income, GA-HD-64 is an educated, economically stable professional constituency that votes Democratic when Democratic governance delivers on education quality, healthcare system performance, infrastructure investment, and environmental protection — not on partisan messaging alone. The incumbent's 69% margin is built on the perception that Baker delivers policy competence on the issues these voters prioritize. Maintaining active focus on education funding, healthcare access quality, transportation infrastructure, and environmental standards keeps this constituency engaged and consolidates the margin." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Economic Opportunity & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "Together, Black constituents (32.28%) and Hispanic/Latino residents (7.74%) represent nearly 40% of the district — large enough that their specific concerns around economic mobility, education equity, healthcare access, and criminal justice are the foundation of the 69% result, not supplementary. The incumbent's margin with these communities is built on responsiveness to their material concerns and the perception that Baker prioritizes their constituents' wellbeing. Active, ongoing constituent engagement on the specific issues that shape economic opportunity in these communities — not ceremonial acknowledgment — determines whether their support remains enthusiastic or erodes." },
          { name: "Renter Population Housing Affordability & Tenant Protection", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.3%, renters constitute more than one-fifth of the district — concentrated in urban areas and composed of young professionals and families for whom housing affordability and tenant protections are material economic concerns distinct from homeowner priorities. While smaller than the homeowner majority, this constituency is large enough to affect turnout and volunteer enthusiasm, and a challenger who campaigns on the incumbent's inattention to renter concerns can create erosion at the margin. The incumbent should engage this constituency's specific housing concerns with policy substance, not dismiss them as marginal or fringe advocacy." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as Permission to Reduce Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 69% margin is built on constituent alignment with the incumbent's specific policy delivery and responsiveness, not on partisan loyalty or default Democratic voting. Any decline in constituent engagement, responsiveness, or focus on the material concerns of the white college-educated base, the Black population, the Hispanic population, or the renter constituency creates an erosion point that a credible challenger can exploit. The margin is strong but not fireproof; it requires active maintenance, not reduced effort." },
          { name: "Assuming the Three Demographic Communities' Concerns Are Interchangeable", tag: "avoid", why: "The college-educated white professional base, the 32.28% Black population, and the 7.74% Hispanic population support the same incumbent for materially different reasons grounded in their specific economic and social concerns. Collapsing these constituencies into a generic 'Democratic coalition' and addressing them with generic messaging risks making each feel taken-for-granted rather than specifically seen. The incumbent's strength comes from demonstrating understanding of and responsiveness to each community's distinct concerns, not from treating them as interchangeable voting blocs." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "GA-HD-64: A 69% Democratic Stronghold with a College-Educated, Racially Diverse, Middle-Class Foundation That Demands Consolidation, Not Expansion.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "GA-HD-64 is a decisively Democratic district in which the incumbent Baker secured 69% of the vote in 2024 — a margin that reflects genuine constituent alignment rather than partisan default. The district's demographic profile explains this result with precision: 52.84% white, 32.28% Black, 7.74% Hispanic, with college-educated adults at 28% (above state average), median household income at $92,338 (solidly upper-middle-class), and median age at 37.7 (established professionals and families, not youth-driven instability). The 21.3% renter rate indicates a community of homeowners with meaningful asset stakes, but renters constitute more than one-fifth of the constituency — large enough to reflect urban professionals and younger families whose housing costs and access concerns shape their political evaluation. This is not a working-class Democratic base held by social conservatism or inherited loyalty; it is an educated, economically stable, racially diverse constituency that votes Democratic because Democratic governance aligns with their material interests and values. The 69% margin is real and durable, but it is not permission to govern on autopilot.",
          "The primary strategic challenge for the incumbent is consolidation and deepening, not expansion into hostile territory. At 69%, Baker has already secured the coalition required to hold the seat; the meaningful political work now is preventing erosion at the margins and ensuring that the three demographic communities (white college-educated professionals, Black constituents, and Hispanic/Latino residents) perceive the representation as substantive and responsive rather than taken-for-granted. The $92,338 income and 28% college attainment signal a constituency that evaluates representation on policy competence, constituent service responsiveness, and alignment with their specific economic and social concerns — not on partisan loyalty alone. The 32.28% Black population and 7.74% Hispanic population together represent nearly 40% of the district; these communities require active, ongoing engagement around specific issues (economic mobility, healthcare access, education quality, housing affordability) rather than ceremonial acknowledgment. The renter population, while smaller than homeowners, is concentrated enough in urban areas to matter for turnout and enthusiasm — and their concerns about housing affordability and tenant protections differ materially from homeowner concerns about property tax fairness and school quality. Losing focus on any of these constituencies' specific concerns creates an opening for a challenger who campaigns on the incumbent's inattention."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Consolidate the college-educated white professional base through policy delivery on the specific economic and social issues that animate this constituency: education quality, healthcare system performance, infrastructure investment, and environmental standards — not through partisan messaging alone.",
          "Maintain active constituent engagement with the 32.28% Black population and 7.74% Hispanic population around economic mobility, education equity, healthcare access, and criminal justice issues; at 69%, these communities are already aligned, but the incumbent's responsiveness to their specific concerns determines whether that alignment remains enthusiastic or becomes perfunctory.",
          "Address the 21.3% renter constituency's housing affordability and tenant protection concerns with policy specificity; renters in a $92,338-median-income district are urban professionals and younger families, not subsistence-level populations — their concerns deserve substantive engagement, not dismissal as fringe advocacy.",
          "Recognize that 69% in a district this educated and engaged is a strong margin held by alignment with constituent interests and values, not a permission structure to govern on reduced constituent engagement; erosion of that margin comes from inattention to any of the three major demographic communities' specific concerns, not from opposition recruitment."
        ]
