          { name: "Treating Unopposed General Election Status as Permanent Security", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% Democratic result with zero Republican opposition can create false confidence that the seat is locked and incumbent protection automatic; in reality, unopposed general elections often precede primary challenges, and a $104K-income, 41%-college-educated constituency is precisely the demographic most likely to produce alternative candidates who believe they can do better constituent service. Treating this seat as safe rather than as a competitive space requiring active engagement is the single most reliable way to generate a primary challenger with stronger community visibility." },
          { name: "Assuming One-Party Districts Require No Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Democratic-dominant districts without credible Republican opposition can drift into passivity where the incumbent assumes demographic alignment equals constituent loyalty; in fact, affluent, educated constituencies with economic stakes in specific policy outcomes (education funding, property values, economic development) evaluate on constituent service quality and policy specificity, not party affiliation. Any incumbent who treats this district as demographically guaranteed rather than as a coalition requiring active, ongoing engagement will find that assumption tested by a primary challenger with stronger policy fluency and visible community presence." }
