          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Economic Mobility & Representation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The Black (21.08%) and Hispanic (6.23%) communities together represent more than one-quarter of GA-HD-72, yet the 75-25 partisan result suggests these communities are either underrepresented in turnout or split in ways that favor the Republican incumbent—both conditions that are contingent, not structural. Workforce development, small business access to capital, housing discrimination, and representation in local hiring are the material issues that organize these communities politically, and a candidate who engages these constituencies with authentic constituent relationship-building rather than transactional outreach can shift their candidate preference by 5-10 percentage points, moving the overall result from 75-25 toward 65-35 or closer." },
          { name: "Renter Community Turnout & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 37.5% renter rate, GA-HD-72 has a larger housing-cost-vulnerable constituency than typical Republican-leaning suburban districts, and renters have historically lower turnout than homeowners but higher political engagement around housing affordability and tenant protection. The incumbent's 75% may rest partly on renter underrepresentation in the actual voting population; a challenger who mobilizes renter turnout around concrete housing affordability issues could shift the overall composition of the electorate in ways that improve their position even if they do not flip the seat outright." },
          { name: "Treating 75% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75-25 result in a district with 37.5% renters, $63,387 median income, 35.1 median age, and a multiracial composition (67.87% white, but more than one-quarter non-white) is not a durable landslide—it is a working-class seat whose margin depends on baseline Republican preference among the white majority and relatively low turnout among the renter and minority communities. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 75% represents a structural Republican hold is strategically blind to the constituency's actual economic vulnerability and demographic composition." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Constituency's Distinct Economic Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "With 37.5% renting, more than one-third of GA-HD-72's households have fundamentally different economic interests than homeowners—no property equity, no stable housing cost, exposure to annual rent increases and eviction risk. A candidate who campaigns this district as though the homeowner majority represents the entire constituency will miss the turnout and persuasion opportunity among renters, who are often politically engaged around housing issues despite lower absolute turnout rates, and whose candidate preference is currently contingent rather than locked in." }
