          { name: "Education Quality and Youth Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.2% college-attainment rate and 36.7-year median age together signal a community where K-12 education quality and the pathway from high school to economic stability are primary family concerns. Parents in HD-87 evaluate their representative substantially on whether schools are adequately funded, whether vocational and workforce development pathways are accessible, and whether young adults from the district have concrete opportunity for stable employment. Visible legislative focus on education funding, workforce development partnerships, and youth economic opportunity creates a constituent relationship that transcends electoral cycle competitiveness." },
          { name: "Unopposed Incumbency and Latent Competitive Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "A 2024 unopposed result in Georgia's current political environment reflects Democratic organization strength but does not eliminate the possibility of future Republican investment or challenger emergence, particularly if national dynamics shift or if local governance performance creates opening. The district's 72% Black composition provides a strong Democratic floor, but that floor is not infinitely stable — Black voter participation and turnout sensitivity to perceived incumbent performance are real variables. An incumbent should treat unopposed status not as confirmation of permanent security, but as an opportunity to build a record of delivery substantial enough to deter credible challenge if Republican recruitment strategies change." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Democratic Without Demonstrated Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed incumbency can create a governance dynamic where an incumbent rests on demographic partisan advantage without building a record of concrete delivery on working-class economic concerns. In a district where 40.6% of households rent and median income is $62,644, a representative who governs from a position of electoral security rather than active constituent focus risks building latent frustration that a credible challenger could activate. Transparent, ongoing demonstration of delivery on material economic concerns is not optional in a working-class district; it is the foundation of durable electoral strength." },
          { name: "Dismissing Republican Competitive Possibility as Demographically Implausible", tag: "avoid", why: "While HD-87's 72% Black composition creates a strong Democratic demographic foundation, dismissing the possibility of Republican competitive investment in future cycles would be strategically naive. Georgia's political environment has demonstrated Republican capacity to mobilize around economic messaging, and a district with a $62,644 median income and working-class material constraints is susceptible to economic populism from any source if the incumbent is perceived as insufficiently focused on constituent welfare. The demographic floor is real; the electoral ceiling is not permanent without demonstrated delivery." }
