          { name: "Active Constituent Service Across All Demographic Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The unopposed 2024 result should not translate into reduced constituent service visibility; it creates a governance obligation to demonstrate active, visible representation across all communities — particularly the 30.12% white minority and the 31.4% renter constituency that may not perceive the incumbent as their representative. A district where the incumbent is invisible to substantial constituencies is a district where a well-organized challenger can build a viable base by simply being present and responsive. Constituent service, visible engagement in community meetings, and rapid response to constituent problems are the foundation of electoral durability in an upper-middle-class district." },
          { name: "Renter vs. Homeowner Economic Interests", tag: "careful", why: "While homeowners are the plurality at 68.6%, the 31.4% renter population represents a meaningful constituency whose housing-affordability concerns may diverge from homeowner property-tax concerns. In an upper-middle-class district, some renters may be younger professionals with education credentials similar to the homeowner majority; others may be service workers or families for whom rent stability is a material economic security issue. The incumbent must avoid allowing renter concerns to be absorbed into generic housing-development messaging and instead address rent affordability and tenant protections as distinct policy concerns for a distinct constituency." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Permission to Reduce Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory is the most dangerous position for an incumbent to misinterpret — it is the absence of opposition, not evidence of unshakeable electoral support. Any representative who uses unopposed status as a reason to reduce constituent service, visibility, or economic-development work is actively creating the conditions for a competitive challenger in 2026. In an upper-middle-class district where voters evaluate on concrete delivery, an incumbent who disappears or becomes seen as taking the seat for granted will find even a modest-quality challenger capable of generating a competitive race." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 30.12% White Minority as a Swing Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In a Black-majority district with a 30% white minority, it is easy for a Democratic incumbent to assume the white minority is non-persuadable and focus exclusively on Black voter mobilization. This is a strategic error in an upper-middle-class district where the white 30% is educated, property-holding, and evaluates on economic security and service delivery — not partisan loyalty. A challenger who successfully peels even 5-10 percentage points from the white minority by demonstrating superior commitment to school funding, infrastructure, and property-tax fairness can create a genuinely competitive race. The white minority must be actively retained, not assumed." }
