        { name: "Growing Hispanic Constituency Engagement & Bilingual Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 10.49% Hispanic, HD-95 has a substantial Latino constituency whose specific needs around labor market access, healthcare, and educational opportunity require proactive, Spanish-language outreach rather than assumption of passive Democratic alignment. In a predominantly Black district with a strong incumbent brand, the Hispanic community can easily become invisible in constituent service—but 10.49% is large enough that organized engagement or organized opposition can shift the competitive dynamics within the district. Building bilingual constituent service capacity, visibility in Hispanic community institutions, and responsiveness to economic mobility concerns for working Hispanic families creates genuine cross-ethnic coalition loyalty unavailable to future challengers who wait to mobilize this constituency against the incumbent." },
        { name: "College-Attainment Gap & Economic Mobility Infrastructure for Non-College Households", tag: "careful", why: "At 36.2% college attainment, HD-95 has a substantial working-class and trades-oriented constituency whose economic mobility pathway is not through four-year degrees but through workforce development, vocational training, apprenticeship access, and small-business entrepreneurship. These households have achieved stability partly through skilled trades and entrepreneurship, not solely through professional degree credentials—and their concerns about credential pathways, small-business regulation, and workforce training investment are materially different from college-educated professional priorities. A representative who focuses exclusively on college-education messaging risks signaling that the working-class majority of the constituency is being governed by priorities they did not ask for." },
        { name: "Treating the 2024 Unopposed Result as Evidence of Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed race in a Black-majority Democratic district is a function of Republican resource allocation and primary-opposition absence in that cycle, not evidence that the incumbent has earned the highest possible constituent trust rating. In a district where homeowners are managing specific property tax and neighborhood investment concerns, and where a 10.49% Hispanic constituency has received uneven constituent service, a future primary challenger who runs on more active community engagement and specific legislative delivery can quickly make the 2024 uncontested result look like complacency rather than dominance." },
        { name: "Positioning on Abstract Policy Rather Than Constituent-Specific Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "The Black homeowner majority in HD-95 evaluates representatives on concrete economic outcomes—property values, school quality, healthcare access, neighborhood public safety—not on alignment with national Democratic policy priorities that may not directly affect their day-to-day economic security. At $86,234 income with 82.7% homeownership, this is a constituency that has reached economic stability and wants active governance to protect and extend it, not rhetorical alignment with national Democratic base priorities disconnected from local material concerns. An incumbent who cannot describe specific legislative accomplishments affecting homeowner economic security, school quality, and healthcare affordability is vulnerable to a challenger who can." }
