          "Build constituency-specific engagement with the Black (28.2%), Hispanic (22.5%), and Asian (10.5%) communities that together represent 61.2% of the district—these are not unified voting blocs but economically diverse families responding to schools, public safety, and whether government is delivering; a candidate who can articulate specific solutions to the economic and civic concerns of each community separately gains credibility across the entire coalition.",
          "Lead with education and property tax messaging for the homeowner-majority, affluent constituency (22.3% renter rate, $95,989 income)—schools drive housing decisions in suburbs with this income profile, and education funding sources matter directly to how families evaluate their economic security; property tax fairness and education quality are the governance deliverables this constituency measures on.",
          "Avoid treating 50% as a sustainable margin—it is a tied outcome that reflects either deep persuadability or divided enthusiasm; any incumbent or challenger who consolidates their existing coalition and ignores the opposition's driving concerns will find 50-50 becomes 45-55 in a district this educated and this diverse."
