          "The strategic vulnerability in HD-118 is not the current Republican margin but the assumption of permanence behind it. The combination of a 25% Black population, a younger-skewing median age, and working-class economic pressures—renters stretched by housing costs, homeowners managing property taxes and service decline—creates a constituency that evaluates representatives on whether they are delivering concrete improvements to quality of life. Black voters in working-class districts like HD-118 are not inherently Democratic; they are voters who will support a Republican who demonstrably addresses their community's specific concerns around healthcare access, economic opportunity, education investment, and public safety. An incumbent who has won 67% while the Black community has experienced material decline in any of these domains is vulnerable to a challenger who offers a different governance posture. The current seat-holder should treat the 25% Black constituency not as a fixed opposition base but as persuadable voters whose support is earned through demonstrable investment in their community's quality of life and direct, ongoing constituent engagement."
