          { name: "Upper-Middle-Class Quality of Life: Education Funding, Healthcare, and Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $70,677 median household income and 33.2% college attainment rate place HD-124 in the upper-middle-class range, substantially above state averages, with a constituency that is property-owning (74.9% homeownership), stable in age (45.0 median), and education-conscious. This demographic profile does not align with ideologically driven voting behavior; instead, it reflects a constituency that evaluates on tangible quality-of-life outcomes — school funding quality, higher education affordability, healthcare costs, and whether government is delivering effective services. A legislative agenda that addresses these material concerns with specificity, rather than defaulting to abstract partisan positioning, better reflects the district's actual demographic interests and is more likely to sustain electoral support across the 66-34 split." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Service and Economic Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 7.34% of the district, the Hispanic community is small but present and merits constituent attention distinct from the larger Black community's priorities. Economic opportunity in service and construction sectors, immigration policy specificity, and whether local institutions provide equitable access are the material concerns for a growing but often-overlooked minority constituency. In a district where the demographic story is often centered on the Black-white divide, the Hispanic community can become invisible — and their constituent needs around economic integration and civic participation are real and warrant specific legislative attention." },
          { name: "Treating the 66% as a Durable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result in a district with 27.44% Black population and a 34% opposition that correlates directly with that population size is not evidence of structural Republican dominance — it may reflect incumbent personal advantage, low Black voter turnout relative to population, or specific candidate positioning that has not yet been tested by a credible alternative. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 66% represents a safe baseline rather than an outcome dependent on active constituent engagement and demographic coalition maintenance is strategically misreading the competitive landscape. In a district this demographically diverse and economically sophisticated, the margin is more fragile than the raw percentage suggests." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic and Black Voters Will Not Organize Alternative Candidacies", tag: "avoid", why: "With a combined 34.78% Hispanic and Black population, these communities represent more than one-third of HD-124's residents and have both the demographic scale and institutional capacity to recruit and support a challenger if they conclude the incumbent is not serving them effectively. The current 66-34 margin reflects the absence of such a candidacy, not proof that these communities lack the organizational capacity to generate one. Any candidate who takes Hispanic and Black voter support for granted, or who assumes these constituencies will not mobilize if given an alternative they perceive as more responsive, is ignoring the district's actual demographic power structure." }
