          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $45,383 median income and 29.7% renter rate, HD-128 is a constituency under direct housing cost pressure where rents are consuming an outsized share of household income and property ownership is largely inaccessible. The 50-50 tie in a 52% Black district suggests that neither candidate is currently breaking decisively on the material economic concerns driving voter behavior. A representative who leads on renter protections, housing affordability policy, and cost-of-living relief for households at this income level activates the economic anxiety that explains the opposition and creates a clear basis for consolidating the Black base while opening persuasion opportunities among white working-class voters facing identical pressures." },
          { name: "Workforce Development and Job Quality for 84% Non-College-Educated Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.1% college attainment rate means 84% of the district's adults do not hold four-year degrees and are reliant on wage-based employment without credential-based mobility. In a $45K median income district, job quality, wage floors, skills training, and pathways to stable employment are the primary mechanisms for economic security. A representative with a concrete legislative agenda on apprenticeships, vocational pathways, living wage standards, and employer accountability for job quality — rather than generic growth rhetoric — directly addresses the economic constraint that explains why a 52% Black district is producing only 50% Democratic performance." },
          { name: "The 50-50 Margin and Its Hidden Drivers", tag: "careful", why: "A tie result in a 52% Black district is anomalous and demands specific diagnostic work to understand whether it reflects lower-than-expected Black turnout, unexpected white support for the opposition, or both. Without identifying the precise source of the gap — whether it's mobilization failure among the Black base, credible white working-class defection, or a combination — any strategy risks misallocating effort and repeating the performance that produced the tie. The diagnostic work required here is granular precinct analysis and constituency depth interviews, not assumption." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging That Divides the Working-Class Base", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 42.98% is white and the median income is $45,383, the white working-class is under identical economic pressure as the Black working-class. A campaign that frames economic concerns through a racial or partisan lens rather than a working-class solidarity lens actively alienates persuadable white voters and reinforces a dynamic where the district remains tied. The housing crisis, healthcare access, and job quality issues affecting Black families in HD-128 are also affecting white families at the same income level; a representative who conflates opposition to economic policy with racial antagonism will struggle to build the cross-racial working-class coalition necessary to move beyond 50%." },
          { name: "Assuming the 50% Result is a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50-50 tie is not a floor — it is an unstable equilibrium where any shift in turnout, candidate credibility, or opposition mobilization can flip the seat. The combination of a 52% Black population, sub-$50K median income, high renter rate, and a low college attainment rate creates both Democratic upside potential (if the Black base mobilizes around economic delivery) and Democratic downside risk (if opposition consolidates white working-class voters or suppresses Black turnout through discouragement about economic performance). Treating 50% as though it is secure or predictable is a strategic error; this is one of the most genuinely competitive seats because the base is persuadable and the opposition is fighting for it actively." }
