          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security and Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 71.3% homeownership, $62,697 median household income, and 40.1-year median age, HD-135 is an asset-holding but income-constrained homeowner constituency for whom property tax burden and school funding adequacy are the primary state-level concerns. These are the material governance issues most likely to activate persuadable voters and most likely to create vulnerability if an incumbent neglects them. Specific legislative performance on property tax relief or school funding can consolidate the homeowner base that currently appears safely Republican." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Constituent Engagement and Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 23.19% Black and 2.04% Hispanic, HD-135 has meaningful minority constituencies whose constituent service needs and political preferences remain entirely invisible because the incumbent ran unopposed in 2024. Whether these communities are currently engaged, alienated, or simply uncontacted by any representative of their own demographic group is an open question—and one that a Democratic challenger will test aggressively. Building authentic constituent relationships with Black and Hispanic households before opposition emerges is both a governance obligation and the primary defense against a competitive challenge." },
          { name: "Renter Population Economic Concerns and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 28.7% renter rate represents younger adults, service-sector workers, and others whose economic concerns and housing affordability constraints may not align with homeowner-focused messaging. At $62.7K median household income district-wide, renters in this district likely face genuine affordability pressure; state-level housing policy, tenant protections, and cost-of-living support are issues that could mobilize renter voters toward opposition if the incumbent is perceived as homeowner-focused. This is not a majority constituency, but it is large enough to shift a competitive race and deserves specific attention." },
          { name: "Treating a 100% Unopposed Result as Evidence of Durable Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in a Democratic no-show is a tactical advantage that reveals nothing about electoral durability in a contested race. If Democratic organization emerges and focuses on school funding, healthcare access, or Black community engagement—all plausible mobilizing issues in a district with meaningful minority population and working-class homeowner base—the 100% result becomes irrelevant. Any incumbent who treats this as evidence of a safe seat and neglects constituent engagement infrastructure is arithmetically wrong." },
          { name: "Assuming Minority Community Inertia or Default Republican Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The 25.23% Black and Hispanic population in a district running 100% unopposed is an uncontacted constituency whose actual voting behavior and candidate preferences are entirely untested. Assuming these communities are locked into opposition or will remain unengaged because no one has organized them is the most likely path to vulnerability. A challenger who invests in real community engagement, consistent candidate presence, and issue focus on healthcare access or school equity could generate substantial persuasion and turnout among constituencies the incumbent may not be actively serving." }
