          { name: "Treating 68% as a Durable Floor Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% result in a working-class district with below-median income, 27.1% renters, and 28.88% Black population is not a protected majority—it is a result that must be actively defended through constituent service and engagement with both the incumbent's base and the opposition's demographic constituencies. Any legislator who campaigns this seat as though 68% represents a stable hold, rather than a coalition that requires continuous engagement with working-class economic concerns and the Black community's governance priorities, risks allowing a credible challenger to mobilize around kitchen-table issues and build an alternative coalition." },
          { name: "Neglecting Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 27.1% renter rate in a district with below-median income means one in four households has direct economic interests in housing costs, tenant protections, and housing policy—issues that are particularly salient for younger adults and lower-income households. An incumbent whose legislative record and constituent service do not address renter economic concerns actively creates a mobilization opportunity for opposition campaigns focused on housing affordability, gentrification, and community displacement. In a district this economically vulnerable, housing is not a secondary issue—it is a primary economic security concern for nearly three in ten households." }
