          { name: "Interpreting Unopposed Results as Permanent Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result in a district this economically comfortable can mask future electoral vulnerability if interpreted as permission to neglect constituent engagement or governance focus. Unopposed elections reflect either genuine security or opposition party resource decisions in a specific cycle — not necessarily permanent structural advantage. A representative who uses an unopposed result as permission to reduce constituent service, neglect minority communities' material concerns, or default to partisan messaging rather than governance competence is creating exactly the conditions where a credible challenger emerges with an opening in 2026 or beyond." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Service & Governance Focus", tag: "avoid", why: "In a college-educated suburban district at $79,950 median income with meaningful demographic diversity, voters evaluate primarily on whether government is functioning competently and serving their material interests — not on partisan messaging or inherited party loyalty. An incumbent who relies on partisan frames rather than demonstrable constituent service, education funding improvement, healthcare affordability action, and responsive governance to Black, Hispanic, and renter communities is ceding the primary evaluative terrain that drives electoral competition in districts this prosperous and educated. Governance competence and constituent responsiveness are the vulnerability-resistant strategy; partisanship is the opening an opponent will exploit." }
