          { name: "Black Community Trust & Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 62.11% Black population in an urban core district, the incumbent's 62% margin is mathematically dependent on Black voter alignment — and that alignment rests on the perception that the representative is fighting for Black community material interests and responsive to constituent needs. This is not a base to be taken for granted; it is a relationship that must be continuously demonstrated through presence in neighborhoods, responsiveness to service requests, and advocacy on issues that Black constituents identify as priorities (education, criminal justice reform, economic opportunity, healthcare). An incumbent who maintains deep organizational relationships through churches and civic groups can consolidate the 62%; one perceived as distant or unresponsive creates space for a challenger to build a primary alternative." },
          { name: "Young Adult Economic Opportunity & Education Pipeline", tag: "careful", why: "At 35.2 median age and 19.9% college attainment, GA-HD-143 contains a significant population of younger adults evaluating whether they can build lives in Atlanta — a demographic that weighs housing affordability, job quality, and education access heavily in their electoral choices. Community college funding, apprenticeship programs, and the visibility of entry-level career pathways are the education issues that matter to this cohort, not abstract K-12 improvement. A challenger who positions themselves as more aggressive on workforce development or education access could mobilize this demographic around an alternative; conversely, an incumbent who delivers specific workforce investment and education advocacy deepens their durability among younger voters." },
          { name: "Mischaracterizing the District as Safely Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 margin is not a supermajority or a durable floor in a district this economically stressed; it reflects current alignment on the perception that the incumbent is delivering or advocating on economic materialism. If a challenger — particularly in a primary context — can credibly position themselves as more aggressive on rents, wages, or healthcare, the 62% is vulnerable. Any incumbent who campaigns as though the seat is safely held rather than actively defending it against more economically aggressive competition risks erosion of the margin." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Over Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is $38,980 and 52.1% of households are renters, voters evaluate representatives primarily on whether they deliver material improvements — lower housing costs, better jobs, healthcare access. Any candidate who leads with abstract policy positioning (immigration, gun control, healthcare in the abstract) rather than concrete advocacy for economic improvement will lose persuasiveness with a constituency that has limited financial margin for error and votes primarily on whether the representative is fighting for their survival. Economic materialism is the language of this district; ideological abstraction is the language of defeat." }
