          { name: "Racial Coalition Maintenance Across Evenly Split Black-White Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 42.45% Black and 45.23% white split with 7.83% Hispanic makes HD-151 genuinely multiracial in a way that requires active engagement with all three communities' distinct concerns rather than generic minority outreach or assumption of inherited support. In a district this evenly balanced, any incumbent who consolidates only one racial community's support while neglecting the others creates an opening for opposition recruitment in the other community—or for lower turnout among a community that feels unseen. Demonstrating concrete constituent service and policy attention to each community's specific interests (employment equity, housing discrimination, immigrant pathway access) is foundational to holding a multiracial district." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory as Electoral Vulnerability Rather Than Strength", tag: "careful", why: "Winning unopposed in 2024 eliminates the electoral pressure that normally forces candidates to demonstrate constituent responsiveness, build local relationships, and articulate a coherent legislative agenda. In a district without inherited partisan advantage—a near-even Black-white split at working-class income—an incumbent who has not actively built genuine constituent relationships risks facing a credible challenger in 2026 who can credibly argue neglect or underperformance. The unopposed result should signal the need for more aggressive constituent engagement, not less; treating it as permission to coast would be strategically catastrophic in a district this competitive." },
          { name: "Assuming Constituent Loyalty Based on Demographics", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 45.23% are white and income is $48,218, assuming that white working-class voters will remain consistently Democratic without specific engagement around economic concerns is a strategic error that has cost Democratic seats across the country. Similarly, assuming Black constituents will turn out at high rates and support the incumbent without specific demonstrations of delivery is to ignore the material reality that Black voters in economically stressed districts evaluate based on whether government is actually improving their lives. Neither racial community owes loyalty; both must be actively earned." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 19.3% College-Educated Community as a Swing Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 19.3% college-educated adults—below state and national averages but meaningful in absolute numbers—HD-151 has a constituency of professionals, educators, healthcare workers, and small business owners who may have different policy priorities (education funding, economic development) than the working-class renter majority. Treating this community as secondary to working-class messaging risks losing persuadable swing voters in a district where Republican recruitment could pitch an alternative vision of economic growth and business-friendly policy. The college-educated minority in a working-class majority district requires specific engagement, not assumption." }
