          { name: "Treating 67% as a Durable Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% margin in a 70.94% Black, 59.4% renter, $46,670-income district is not a structural supermajority — it is a contested margin in a constituency where material economic concerns drive voter behavior more than inherited partisan loyalty. The 33% opposition includes both Republican voters unlikely to flip and Democratic-leaning or independent voters frustrated by the pace of change on housing, jobs, or services. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 67% in this district represents a safe floor rather than a contested result requiring active constituent engagement and continued delivery on material economic concerns risks erosion in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 23.1% College-Educated Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "While 23.1% college attainment is below state average, it represents roughly one-quarter of the district's adults — a meaningful cohort of teachers, healthcare workers, social service professionals, and government employees for whom education policy, student debt, and public-sector compensation are direct professional concerns. In a district where the dominant narrative focuses on income and housing struggles, ignoring the civic infrastructure role that college-educated professionals play in community institutions and their specific needs around education funding and professional opportunity creates an opportunity for opposition to recruit this constituency as underappreciated and overlooked." }
