          { name: "Young Adult Economic Mobility & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 29.6 median age and 47.3% renter rate, GA-HD-168 is a district of young adults navigating the transition to economic independence without the wealth-building infrastructure (homeownership, credential-based wages) that anchors older constituencies. Housing costs, entry-level wage trajectories, and access to affordable childcare are the material economic concerns that evaluate incumbent performance for this demographic. Visible legislative effort on housing policy, minimum wage standards, and workforce development infrastructure directly addresses the economic anxiety that shapes how young renters in this district perceive political representation." },
          { name: "Black Community Constituent Service & Political Voice", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 44.67% Black population in a heavily Democratic district, the Black community is the electoral foundation and the primary constituency for whom the representative is accountable. Service delivery through community institutions—churches, civic organizations, social service providers—and genuine responsiveness to concerns about criminal justice, workplace equity, and neighborhood investment are the mechanisms through which Black constituents evaluate whether representation is authentic or performative. Unopposed incumbency can create vulnerability here if service delivery atrophies or constituent relationships become distant." },
          { name: "Maintaining Constituency Engagement Despite Unopposed Status", tag: "careful", why: "Unopposed victory eliminates competitive pressure but creates the risk of reduced constituent visibility and accountability—particularly in a district where young renters and working-class Black voters have limited alternative options but also have material economic concerns that require active legislative attention. The gap between electoral safety and actual constituent satisfaction can widen invisibly until opposition emerges; maintaining visible constituent presence and delivering concrete results is essential to preventing this vulnerability from becoming actionable." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Incumbency as Evidence of Durable Support", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed status in GA-HD-168 likely reflects Republican strategic withdrawal from a heavily Democratic urban district, not evidence of incumbent strength or constituency satisfaction. The demographic profile—young, renting, working-class, with constrained income and limited education credentials—is precisely the profile that becomes electorally volatile if opposition emerges offering economic alternatives. Complacency based on unopposed status can obscure the underlying economic vulnerability of the voter base." },
          { name: "Treating Economic Constraints as Permanent Constituency Facts Rather Than Governance Challenges", tag: "avoid", why: "A $59,120 median income, 19.7% college attainment, and 47.3% renter rate are not immutable demographic characteristics—they reflect the material outcome of policy choices around housing, education, wages, and workforce development. An incumbent who treats these constraints as fixed conditions of urban life rather than as failures of governance to create economic opportunity will find credibility eroding if opposition campaigns on the possibility of different outcomes. Framing economic constraints as governance problems to be solved, rather than constituency characteristics to be managed, is essential to defending against economic-focused challenger campaigns." }
