        { tag: "lean-into", name: "Working-Class Economic Security in a Low-Income, High-Renter District", why: "With $51,547 median income, 16.3% college attainment, and 36.7% renter rate, HD-172 is a working-class district with limited asset accumulation and real economic constraints. Housing affordability, healthcare access, and agricultural employment stability are material concerns shared by white, Black, and Hispanic working-class families who constitute the district's majority. Legislative work on rural hospital funding, agricultural economic development, and housing affordability serves the constituency's actual material interests and demonstrates that constituent service extends beyond the white homeowner core to the working-class majority." },
        { tag: "careful", name: "The Unopposed Result in a Near-Majority-Minority District", why: "The combined Black (24.91%) and Hispanic (16.71%) population constitutes 41.62% of HD-172 — approaching majority-minority composition — yet the district produced an unopposed Republican result. This requires strategic explanation: either the incumbent has built extraordinary constituent credibility across racial lines, or the Democratic Party lacks organizational capacity to recruit a viable candidate. If the latter, the seat is vulnerable to rapid competitive shift when opposition recruitment improves or a credible candidate with community credibility in the Black and Hispanic communities enters the race." },
        { tag: "avoid", name: "Assuming a 42% Combined Minority Population Will Remain Electorally Inactive", why: "A district where 24.91% Black and 16.71% Hispanic residents together constitute 41.62% of the population is not inherently safe for a Republican running unopposed; it is a district where minority voter disengagement is currently the structural condition enabling the uncontested result. Changes in minority community organization — a credible Black candidate with church network support, or a Spanish-speaking Democrat with agricultural community credibility — can rapidly shift the competitive calculus. The incumbent who does not actively engage and serve these communities is building the very opposition that will eventually materialize." },
        { tag: "avoid", name: "Treating Low Educational Attainment as a Constituency Characteristic Rather Than a Governance Failure", why: "At 16.3% college attainment — equal to the lowest in this analysis — HD-172's educational constraint represents a policy failure, not an immutable constituency fact. Young adults in this district face real barriers to workforce credential attainment and economic mobility that state education and job training policy can directly address. Any representative who treats low educational attainment as simply what this constituency is, rather than as a condition that investment in community college access and vocational training can improve, communicates to working-class families that their children's economic futures are not a legislative priority." }
