          { name: "Suburban Homeowner Economic Security and Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $70,805 median income with 75.4% homeownership, GA-HD-175 is a suburban middle-class constituency whose economic comfort is contingent on stable housing costs, property tax management, and education funding that supports home values. With a 36-year median age and predominantly white homeowner base, this constituency is acutely sensitive to fiscal governance, school quality, and whether local services are sustainable. The 72% margin reflects consolidated homeowner alignment, but that consolidation depends on demonstrable delivery on the material concerns that make suburban home ownership viable." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Growth and Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 6.32% of the district, the Hispanic population is small but not negligible, and Hispanic growth in Georgia suburban districts has been consistent over the past decade. In a district voting 72-28 Republican, the Hispanic community's voting patterns and whether they perceive representation in local governance is a potential swing factor in future cycles. Specific attention to Hispanic small-business access to services, workforce development, and family-serving infrastructure — rather than treating Hispanic constituents as invisible in a heavily white homeowner district — demonstrates inclusive governance and maintains alignment." },
          { name: "Running Ideological or Culture-War Messaging That Alienates the 28% Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72-28 margin can create false confidence that the opposition is negligible, but that 28% represents roughly one-quarter of the district and, in suburban Atlanta, likely includes college-educated professionals, public employees, and younger households whose economic interests align with the incumbent party but whose social or fiscal priorities may differ. Governance framing that positions education, healthcare, or public-sector workforce issues as ideological battlegrounds rather than constituent-service questions risks activating opposition that would otherwise remain demobilized." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Demographically Static or Safe from Demographic Change", tag: "avoid", why: "GA-HD-175's demographic profile — 64.7% white, 23.2% Black, 6.3% Hispanic — shows suburban Atlanta's ongoing racial and ethnic diversification. A representative who campaigns as though the 72-28 margin reflects a permanent Anglo homeowner supermajority is vulnerable to the specific dynamic that has shifted dozens of Atlanta suburban seats in recent cycles: when candidates take existing constituencies for granted, demographic growth and turnout mobilization among younger and minority voters creates unexpected competitive pressure. Authentic constituent engagement across racial and ethnic lines is both good governance and good electoral strategy." }
