          { name: "Treating Working-Class Voters as an Inherited Democratic Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "White working-class voters at $65,259 income with 39% renters have demonstrated in multiple states and cycles that they do not vote by demographic destiny—they vote on whether they perceive representation is delivering on their material concerns around housing, healthcare, wages, and local economic opportunity. An incumbent whose strategy assumes this 58.72% will remain Democratic-aligned without visible legislative outcomes on working-class economic issues is making a fundamental strategic error that opponents will exploit relentlessly. The constituency that delivered the 100% unopposed result in 2024 is the same constituency a challenger will target in the next cycle if it perceives the incumbent has not delivered." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 31.5% College-Educated Minority as a Distinct Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The 31.5% college-educated adults in this district represent professionals, educators, and service workers who may have different priorities than the working-class majority—access to quality schools for their children, infrastructure investment, professional workforce development, and neighborhood amenities can be distinct from basic working-family economic security concerns. An incumbent whose legislative agenda focuses exclusively on baseline working-class issues while ignoring professional-class priorities creates an opening for a challenger who can credibly argue for a broader governing vision. These are not separate constituencies; they are overlapping communities whose different concerns require deliberate attention rather than assuming a single economic message addresses all." }
