          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Electoral Safety Without Constituent Work", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% Democratic result in 2024 creates a strategic illusion of durable safety that can lead to reduced constituent engagement and legislative visibility—precisely the conditions that make a previously safe seat vulnerable when a credible challenger emerges. Unopposed victories are politically advantageous but organizationally dangerous if the incumbent interprets them as permission to reduce constituent services, legislative output visibility, or district presence. A working-class constituency with median income of $64,727 and 31.7% renters will not sustain loyalty to an incumbent who appears to be taking their support for granted." },
          { name: "Assuming Democratic Turnout Will Hold Without Active Mobilization", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed 2024 result reflects baseline Democratic performance in a heavily Democratic district, not a ceiling on what the incumbent can accomplish or evidence that turnout will remain stable in the absence of active constituent engagement. In a working-class, mixed-race district where material economic concerns are the primary voting driver, turnout and electoral performance are sensitive to whether voters perceive government as delivering on economic security. An incumbent who allows organizational infrastructure to atrophy or reduces investment in community engagement will find that baseline performance corrodes when economic conditions tighten or a challenger emerges with a more compelling economic message." }
