          { name: "Black Community Engagement & Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31.91% Black population is substantial enough that African American voter participation and satisfaction is essential to holding the district; unopposed victory in 2024 provides no data on how effectively the incumbent is serving Black constituents on the issues that matter most to that community. Education funding, criminal justice reform, healthcare equity, and economic opportunity are the policy areas where Black voters evaluate performance, and an incumbent must demonstrate active engagement and specific policy outcomes in these domains rather than assuming that Democratic party affiliation alone ensures Black voter loyalty. Without visible Black community outreach and service, an incumbent risks appearing to take this crucial constituency for granted." },
          { name: "Unopposed Vulnerability & Incumbent Complacency", tag: "careful", why: "Watson's unopposed 2024 victory is a structural advantage but also a diagnostic liability: without visible opposition, the incumbent has no data on where the district's actual vulnerabilities lie or how voters would respond to a credible challenger. A constituency at $52,851 income with 17.3% college attainment is responsive to clear economic messaging, and an incumbent who assumes safe tenure without active constituent engagement risks being surprised by opposition when it emerges. The 39.0 median age and 35.9% renter rate suggest younger and less-affluent voters whose loyalty cannot be taken for granted in the absence of demonstrated incumbent service and responsiveness." },
          { name: "Ideological Messaging Over Economic Concreteness", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 17.3% college attainment and $52,851 median income does not evaluate primarily on abstract ideological positioning; abstract Democratic messaging about values or identity, unaccompanied by specific proposals for cost-of-living relief and economic opportunity, will underperform with this audience. An incumbent who campaigns on ideological grounds rather than concrete economic service will appear disconnected from the material concerns that actually structure voters' lives and will be vulnerable to a challenger offering clearer economic messaging." },
          { name: "Treating Black Community as Assumed Loyalty Rather Than Active Constituent Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The 31.91% Black population is large enough to be decisive in a Democratic district, but it is not monolithic in its political expectations or immune to perceptions of neglect. An incumbent who campaigns in Black neighborhoods only during election cycles, or who defaults to generic Democratic messaging rather than specific policy engagement on education, justice, and economic equity, risks Black voter demobilization or openness to alternative candidates who offer more visible community investment. This constituency requires year-round constituent service and clearly demonstrated policy outcomes, not periodic campaign attention." }
