          { name: "Partisan Polarization as a Substitute for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 margin in an educated, affluent district is vulnerable to erosion if the incumbent substitutes partisan rhetoric for direct engagement on the material concerns of school funding, healthcare costs, and local service delivery. College-educated voters in affluent suburbs are pragmatic about governance competence; they will support Republicans who deliver results but will shift toward Democrats if they perceive partisan positioning taking precedence over constituent problem-solving." },
          { name: "Treating the 34% Opposition as a Permanent Minority Rather Than as Swing Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "A one-third Democratic baseline in an educated, diverse suburban district is a meaningful opposition with organizational capacity and demographic roots in college-educated, professional, and minority communities—not a permanent minority but a constituency capable of becoming a majority if the incumbent fails to maintain constituent relationships or if economic conditions shift. Any candidate who campaigns as though the 66% is secure while neglecting to engage the opposition directly risks being surprised by shifts in school-board funding, healthcare policy, or local economic conditions that mobilize the opposition base." }
