          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe, Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 margin in a district where 41.2% is Black or Hispanic, 29.6% is renters, and 81.4% is non-college-educated is not a safe majority—it is a consolidation of the white homeowner constituency in an otherwise diverse and economically vulnerable district. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as a safe hold, or who assumes the opposition is unmovable, is ignoring the actual composition of the electorate and the material concerns driving it. This is a seat that requires active constituent engagement on economic issues, not passive reliance on partisan registration." },
          { name: "Prioritizing Abstract Partisan or Cultural Issues Over Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "At 18.6% college attainment and $59,605 median income, voters in GA-SD-23 are most responsive to messaging about their material circumstances—wage growth, healthcare costs, housing affordability—not to abstract cultural or partisan positioning. A legislator whose record emphasizes cultural or partisan issues over concrete economic delivery, or whose rhetoric suggests indifference to working-class economic concerns, is messaging in a language that this constituency does not prioritize. In a district this economically constrained, material concerns trump partisan loyalty." }
