          { name: "Education Quality & Funding for Professional Families", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 32.3% college attainment—above Georgia's state average—and $80,053 median household income, GA-SD-24 is anchored by professional families whose primary government expenditure concern is education quality. School funding adequacy, teacher recruitment and retention, and curriculum rigor are the material governance issues that drive persuadable voters in this district. A legislative record that prioritizes education investment and quality signals to this constituency that the incumbent is aligned with their values and concerns; ceding this issue to Democratic framing invites a credible challenger to establish dominance on the issue most important to high-income families." },
          { name: "Responsive Constituent Service Across All Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 18.9% Black population and combined 9.3% Hispanic and Asian population represent nearly 28% of the district's constituency, and these communities in an affluent suburb evaluate representation on concrete constituent service and whether their economic mobility and civic concerns receive active legislative attention. An unopposed election can create complacency about whether minority constituencies feel genuinely represented or simply tolerated; a challenger who invests in intensive community engagement with these populations can credibly claim to offer more responsive representation. The unopposed 2024 margin masks the reality that building durable support among diverse affluent suburbs requires continuous visible engagement, not periodic attention." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter-Community Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.4% renters, GA-SD-24 includes young professionals and minority families for whom housing costs are a primary economic stress in an affluent district where home prices may be escalating faster than incomes. While renters are a minority of the constituency, they are politically volatile in suburban districts—a legislator whose record focuses exclusively on property-tax protection for homeowners while ignoring rental market dynamics or affordable-housing accessibility signals to renters and young families that the incumbent does not represent their economic interests. In a district with demographic drift toward greater diversity, renter concerns deserve specific policy attention rather than assumption that homeowner priorities are universal." },
          { name: "Partisan Mobilization Messaging to Rural Republican Base", tag: "avoid", why: "GA-SD-24's demographic and economic profile—affluent, educated, increasingly diverse, suburban—is precisely the district where partisan mobilization rhetoric and culture-war framing are most likely to alienate persuadable voters. An incumbent who campaigns on abstract partisan identity and culture-war concerns rather than education quality, constituent service, and responsive governance is ceding the terrain where this district's college-educated and minority constituencies make their voting decisions. In a district that went unopposed in 2024 but faces demographic drift, partisan messaging is a strategically risky foundation for the next cycle." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Permission to Govern Narrowly", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election is not a mandate to focus exclusively on homeowner priorities or the Anglo majority; it is an opportunity to strengthen the base and expand persuadability among constituencies that might vote for a credible alternative in a competitive cycle. An incumbent who treats unopposed status as permission to neglect renter communities, minority constituencies, or education quality—the issues that drive persuadable suburban voters—is allowing structural vulnerabilities to compound before the next challenge emerges. The strategic task is to use the unopposed period to strengthen constituent relationships across all communities and to establish a legislative record that makes a challenger's argument for change less credible, not to assume that 2024's margin is durable in 2026 or beyond." }
