          { name: "Education Quality and School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "GA-SD-28 is a college-educated (32%), affluent ($91,273 median income), stable-homeowner (77.6%) suburban constituency whose property values, community identity, and future investment are directly tied to school performance and funding. For this demographic, education quality is not an abstract social priority—it is a material household investment decision. A state senator who leads on education funding and can credibly claim to have delivered improved school resources and outcomes has aligned themselves with the constituency's most direct material interest." },
          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Economic Security and Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 77.6% homeownership and $91,273 median income, GA-SD-28 is a constituency of asset-holding suburban professionals for whom property taxes, transportation infrastructure, growth management, and neighborhood livability are urgent practical concerns. Rising property taxes, traffic congestion, overcrowded schools from rapid development, and aging infrastructure create direct economic pressure on household budgets and quality of life. A legislator who delivers on infrastructure investment, manages growth thoughtfully, and addresses property tax fairness serves this constituency's material needs more directly than abstract partisan messaging." },
          { name: "Black Constituency Representation and Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "At 20.07% of the district, the Black constituency is substantial enough to be a core Democratic voter but not large enough to sustain the seat if white suburban voters shift—meaning that targeted, authentic engagement with Black constituents on criminal justice, economic opportunity, and community investment is both a governance obligation and an electoral necessity. The unopposed 2024 result should not breed complacency; demonstrating that you are serving this constituency with specific attention to their priorities, not treating them as guaranteed votes, builds trust and turnout." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed as Evidence of Durable Security", tag: "avoid", why: "The absence of Republican opposition in 2024 is not evidence that GA-SD-28 is safely Democratic; it is evidence that Republicans have judged the seat not worth contesting in the current cycle. The district's 66.77% white, college-educated, affluent suburban demographic is precisely the swing constituency that has shifted decisively toward Democrats since 2016 but remains capable of shifting back if given sufficient reason. Governing as though 100% is a floor rather than a current status quo invites the complacency that allows a well-funded challenger to gain unexpected traction." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "GA-SD-28's affluent suburban constituency evaluates primarily on governance delivery—school quality, infrastructure, property tax fairness, public safety—not on partisan identity or national talking points. A state senator who leads with partisan messaging rather than specific accomplishments on the material issues that affect household budgets and neighborhood quality is ceding the political terrain to any challenger who can credibly argue that you have failed to deliver. In a district this affluent and educated, constituent voters are sophisticated enough to distinguish between talk and governance results." }
