          { name: "Treating 65% as an Inherently Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65% result in a district where 27.92% is Black, 33.2% are renters, and 30.8% are college-educated is not a landslide born of universal approval — it reflects strong alignment with a specific white homeowner coalition whose satisfaction can be conditional. If that coalition experiences economic stress (property taxes, healthcare costs) or perceives that public investments are not benefiting their concerns, the 65% floor is not as durable as raw numbers suggest. Conversely, if the renter and Black communities perceive genuine neglect or a challenger offers more direct engagement, a 65% margin can compress faster than a representative confident in its permanence would anticipate." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 35% Opposition as Locked-In Democratic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The 35% opposition in GA-SD-29 is not an immovable base of committed Democrats — it is rooted in real demographic groups (27.92% Black, 33.2% renters) with material governance concerns around housing, healthcare, and economic opportunity. Treating this 35% as irredeemable opposition, rather than as constituents whose concerns can be addressed or whose support can be earned through specific policy attention, misses both the actual composition of the opposition and the potential for a challenger to move the margin by offering more direct engagement with those constituencies' real needs." }
