          "Build active relationships with Black, Hispanic, and Asian constituencies rather than assuming their votes; in an unopposed cycle these communities' specific concerns may not have surfaced as visible policy priorities, and a challenger with credible engagement could discover unmet needs or exploit service gaps.",
          "Lead with competence and specific policy outcomes on issues that matter to professional-class voters: education quality and funding, healthcare system stability, property tax fairness, and infrastructure; affluent suburbs vote on these material concerns, not primarily on partisan identity.",
          "Treat the 35% non-white constituency as a core part of the district rather than a secondary concern; at one-third of the population, authentic service to Black, Hispanic, and Asian families is a governance obligation and a political necessity in a district where opposition could organize around unmet needs."
