          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 28.3% renters in a median-income district of $102,387, GA-SD-32 has a substantial cohort of mobile professionals and service workers whose economic exposure to housing costs is direct and immediate. Unlike homeowners insulated by long-term fixed mortgages, renters are exposed to market-rate volatility, and a 43.8% college-educated workforce likely includes renters earning professional salaries but facing real affordability pressure in the current market. Any candidate who addresses housing supply, zoning policy, or occupational licensing barriers to affordability with specificity can activate this constituency on material self-interest rather than partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Cost-of-Living and Affluent Professional Class Material Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $102,387 median income and 43.8% college attainment define a constituency of affluent professionals for whom the question is not whether government delivers basic services but whether state policy creates friction or opportunity in their economic lives. Tax burden, regulatory efficiency, and quality-of-life services (schools, parks, transit) are the concrete governance measures this constituency uses to evaluate representatives. An incumbent or challenger who leads with specific policy outcomes on these material concerns rather than abstract partisan messaging will command credibility with voters sophisticated enough to distinguish between rhetoric and results." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity and Minority Community Specific Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "With 11.63% Hispanic and 4.72% Asian populations in a 63.88% white, affluent, suburban district, the minority communities here are integrated into the professional class rather than concentrated in separate economic zones. This integration means that economic messaging on housing, tax burden, and opportunity can reach these communities authentically—but it also means that minority communities can become invisible if represented through generic diversity language rather than specific attention to how housing costs, occupational licensing, or tax policy affect them as professionals. Authentic engagement requires recognizing these voters as members of the affluent professional constituency, not as separate constituent groups." },
          { name: "Relying on 2024 Unopposed Status as Evidence of Structural Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "The incumbent's unopposed 2024 result reflects the specific dynamics of that cycle—Republican candidate recruitment challenges, Democratic resource allocation priorities, or the absence of a credible challenger—not fundamental structural Democratic dominance in GA-SD-32. In a district with a meaningful renter plurality exposed to cost pressures and an affluent professional base that evaluates on material governance rather than inherited partisan loyalty, a well-resourced challenger with a credible economic message could quickly erode this margin. Treating the unopposed result as evidence of a safe seat creates vulnerability to being outflanked on the issues that actually move these voters." },
          { name: "Assuming Affluence = Democratic Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "The post-2016 suburban realignment produced Democratic gains among affluent, college-educated voters—but this represents a coalition shift, not a locked-in demographic bloc. Affluent professionals evaluate on material governance outcomes, and if they perceive that state policy is creating friction (housing costs, tax burden, regulatory burden) or that a challenger offers better governance solutions, party affiliation is secondary to self-interest. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes affluence correlates with durable Democratic support will be surprised by how quickly material concerns can override inherited partisan patterns in a college-educated, economically sophisticated constituency." }
