          { name: "Renter Stability & Affordable Housing Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.6% renter rate and $65,901 median income, housing burden is the dominant material concern for a plurality of constituents whose rent consumes a disproportionate share of household income. Tenant protections, affordable housing development through zoning and public-private partnership, eviction prevention, and anti-displacement policy in gentrifying south DeKalb are the legislative priorities that translate Democratic alignment into material benefit. An incumbent without a visible record on housing affordability is vulnerable to primary challenge from a candidate who leads on this issue." },
          { name: "Job Training, Workforce Development & Wage-Level Employment", tag: "lean-into", why: "A working-class constituency at $65,901 median income with 33.5% college attainment needs legislative attention to job training partnerships, apprenticeship programs, and wage-level employment pathways — particularly in healthcare, skilled trades, and public-sector positions. The incumbent's relationship with community colleges, workforce development boards, and employers in the district determines whether constituents see pathways for economic mobility or perceive themselves as locked in low-wage service work." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Medicaid Sustainability", tag: "careful", why: "A renter-majority, modest-income constituency faces real healthcare access constraints and Medicaid coverage is a material issue for working families with unstable employment. The incumbent's position on Medicaid expansion, rural hospital viability, and healthcare affordability carries weight in district perception, particularly among families choosing between healthcare and rent, and any perceived ambiguity on these issues can activate primary opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 82% as a Permanent Coalition Rather Than Current Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "An 82% partisan result reflects baseline alignment with Democratic governance on race and economic fairness, not a permanent coalition immune to erosion; without visible delivery on material economic conditions, the margin can contract to 75-78% in a subsequent cycle or face primary challenge from a candidate perceived as more responsive to constituent economic conditions." },
          { name: "Generic Equity Messaging Without Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class constituency where rent burden and wage constraints are daily realities, abstract rhetoric about equity and justice without legislative record on housing, employment, and healthcare translates as political theater rather than representation; constituents evaluate on whether their material conditions improve, and an incumbent who claims alignment without delivery risks primary opposition or suppressed turnout in special elections." }
