          { name: "White Homeowner Base Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 63% Republican margin in GA-SD-47 is built on the foundation of the 65% white, homeowner-heavy constituency with above-average income and education. This base is stable if actively engaged through property-tax policy, school funding support, and messaging that reinforces their material interests in neighborhood investment and economic continuity. An incumbent who loses focus on explicit homeowner-benefit messaging risks baseline erosion among the exact constituency that produces the 63% margin." },
          { name: "The 13.2% Demographic Gap and Hidden Constituencies", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 86.8% of GA-SD-47's population, leaving 13.2% unaccounted for — likely Hispanic, Asian, Native American, or multiracial residents whose geographic concentration, age profile, and political orientation are unknown. In a district winning 63-37, this gap represents a constituency that is currently either not voting at high rates, aligned with the Republican incumbent, or organizing under the visibility threshold. If a Democratic challenger targets this population specifically with culturally-resonant messaging and mobilization, the gap could become a strategic vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Durable Safe Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 result is comfortable but not invulnerable, particularly in a district with 35.9% renters, 35.6 median age, and 13.2% demographic gap. If an incumbent campaigns this seat as though 63% represents a stable coalition and reduces constituent engagement, a challenger who mobilizes young professionals, educated renters, and the hidden demographic gap could plausibly move the margin to 58-60% or closer. This is a seat that demands sustained engagement with the persuadable, not quiet confidence in the existing margin." },
          { name: "Demographic & Economic Blind Spots", tag: "avoid", why: "An incumbent strategy that focuses exclusively on messaging to white homeowners and assumes the renter and minority populations will either vote Republican or remain disengaged misses the actual constituency composition of the district. If a challenger frames the race around the material economic concerns of younger adults and minorities — housing costs, healthcare access, economic opportunity — and the incumbent's response is generic conservative ideology rather than specific constituent-benefit messaging, the gap between the 37% opposition and the 35.9% renter rate will widen." }
