          { name: "Middle-Class Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "GA-SD-49's median household income of $78,176 and 27.7% college attainment rate profile a district of middle-class working and professional families whose economic stability depends on housing affordability, stable employment, and accessible services. The 31.4% renter rate—substantially above the national 35% homeownership floor—signals a constituency that includes both renters facing rising housing costs and moderate-income homeowners managing mortgage obligations in an inflationary environment. A legislative agenda that treats housing affordability, workforce development, and property tax management as core governance priorities speaks directly to the material concerns of a district where household income is modest relative to coastal metros but real for the families living there, and where economic anxiety about cost-of-living increases is acute." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Community Cohesion", tag: "careful", why: "GA-SD-49 is a genuinely diverse district where white residents (60.44%) are not a dominant supermajority, Hispanic residents comprise more than a quarter of the population, and Black and Asian residents bring additional community stakes and representation needs. The unopposed 2024 result masks the possibility that this diversity has not yet sorted into stable partisan coalition—Hispanic voters in Georgia have shown sensitivity to both immigration policy and economic opportunity messaging, and in a district where the incumbent faced no opposition, the actual preference distribution among these communities remains unknown. Any candidate who assumes that the current electoral result reflects stable multi-racial coalition support risks misreading the district's actual political terrain and facing a credible challenger if a future opponent mobilizes Hispanic or other minority voters around specific economic or cultural concerns." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Electoral Durability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed general election result reveals nothing about electoral strength or constituent satisfaction—only that no organized opposition candidate emerged in 2024. In a district with 26.92% Hispanic population, 31.4% renter rate, and a middle-class income profile where household economic security is non-trivial, the absence of opposition in a single cycle should not be mistaken for political invulnerability. A challenger who runs a credible campaign focused on housing affordability, economic opportunity for Hispanic residents, or middle-class cost-of-living concerns could find genuine electoral terrain if the incumbent has not built authentic constituent relationships across the district's diverse communities." },
          { name: "Assuming Incumbent Brand Without Testing Constituent Knowledge", tag: "avoid", why: "Because the 2024 election was unopposed, the incumbent's visibility, constituent approval, or brand recognition in GA-SD-49 is not measured by any electoral data. Without baseline polling or constituent awareness metrics, it is dangerous to assume that an unopposed win reflects high approval or strong relationship with the district's actual communities—particularly among Hispanic residents (26.92%) or renters (31.4%) who may not have organized political engagement pathways to the incumbent. Any strategic planning that assumes the incumbent's position is secure without testing actual constituent sentiment risks ignoring a vulnerability that a well-organized challenger could exploit." }
