          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.9 median age, 73.5% homeownership, and $59,977 income, GA-SD-53 is a district of middle-class homeowners entering peak financial pressure years where property taxes, healthcare costs, and infrastructure quality determine perceived economic security. The 21% Democratic opposition in a demographically favorable Republican district suggests that cost-of-living anxieties are not being adequately addressed by current representation. Specific legislative accomplishment on property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and school funding is the currency that maintains and reinforces the 79% coalition rather than allowing opposition to organize around kitchen-table economics." },
          { name: "Blue-Collar Workforce Economic Mobility & Skills Training", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.8% college attainment rate is substantially below state and national averages, indicating a trades-oriented and blue-collar workforce with real economic vulnerability to automation, outsourcing, and wage stagnation. This constituency's economic trajectory is not secured by education credentials but by access to stable employment, wage growth, and workforce development opportunities that keep their skills marketable. A representative who engages manufacturing, construction, and service-sector workforce development with specificity — apprenticeships, community college funding, industry partnership — addresses the material economic anxiety that explains why a Republican district is only 79% instead of 88%." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Potential Underserved Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories sum to 96.37%, leaving a ~3.6% gap that, while smaller than in more rural districts, may represent Native American, immigrant, or other communities whose constituent needs are not being systematically addressed. If these communities exist and are receiving less service attention proportionally than the white homeowner majority, they represent both a governance failure and a latent source of opposition mobilization if a credible challenger offers better engagement." },
          { name: "Treating 79% as a Permission Structure for Neglecting Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 79% result is comfortable but not dominant, and it represents a 6+ point underperformance versus Republican performance in demographically comparable districts — a gap that indicates constituent concerns are not being adequately addressed. Any representative who interprets the 79% margin as permission to ignore the 21% opposition and rely on inherited Republican loyalty without active engagement on cost-of-living and economic mobility issues will find that coalition eroding toward a genuinely competitive 60-65% result when opposition momentum builds." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Abstract Conservative Rhetoric Without Economic Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "GA-SD-53's homeowner and blue-collar majority are not ideologically rigid; they are economically motivated and will support whoever credibly addresses their material concerns about property taxes, healthcare costs, wage growth, and service quality. A representative who relies on conservative ideology and national partisan messaging without offering specific legislative answers to these local economic concerns is vulnerable to a challenger who leads with concrete solutions on the issues that actually determine whether a 40-year-old homeowner feels financially secure." }
